        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
A rotating space vehicle could be used to generate 'artificial gravity' during long-duration missions, however this would have side effects.  Movements made during body rotation would generate transient Coriolis forces that act perpendicular to both the rotation axis and the movement direction.  Such Coriolis forces initially deviate the trajectories and endpoints of reaching movements, but adaptation occurs to restore accuracy if exposure continues.  The patterns of movement deviation generated by Coriolis forces differ from those observed when movements are perturbed by external, mechanical contact forces of comparable timing and magnitude.  This implies that cutaneous contact cues are critical in the control and monitoring of movement endpoint, trajectory, and adaptation.  The goal of this research was to investigate the conjoint influence on reaching and adaptation of cutaneous sensory signals, proprioceptors, and efferent commands.  The effect on reaching movements of exposure to contact force perturbations, non-contact Coriolis forces, and combinations of the two were measured.  Acquisition, retention, and transfer of adaptation was also studied.<p>
	
APPROACH:<BR>
Experiments were conducted using the Slow Rotation Room Facility located at the Ashton Graybiel Spatial Orientation Laboratory, Brandeis University, Waltham, MA.<p>

RESULTS:<br>
The results of these experiments have shown the following: 1) reaching movements to targets are deviated by Coriolis forces and mechanical contact forces; 2)  both contact and non-contact perturbations generate errors in reaching paths and endpoints in the direction of the perturbing forces; 3) adaptation of movement endpoint and movement path are dissociable; 4) endpoint adaptation occurs rapidly with additional movements even in the absence of visual error feedback so long as the hand makes contact with a surface at the end of the movement; 5) there are no aftereffects from adaptation to mechanically induced errors when the subjects' arms are no longer attached to the apparatus, however,  after adaptation to Coriolis force perturbations when the slow rotation room was again at rest subjects showed large after-effects in pointing to targets; 6) there is partial cross-transfer between inertial Coriolis force perturbations and mechanical contact force perturbations, this indicates that adaptation to mechanical contact forces is context specific; and 7) If the hand is maintained in contact with a surface while moving to point to a target during rotation, adaptation to the Coriolis force will occur for movement trajectory but not movement endpoint if visual feedback is denied.<P>
This research has enlightened the scientific community about the presence of Coriolis and other inertial accelerations on the arms and legs during self-generated, voluntary movements in a normal terrestrial environment.  This research has also helped to provide a basis for anticipating and solving potential performance problems in a rotating artificial gravity environment.<end>
        </div>

